IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `soga-nouns`
IMPORT `soga-part1-application`
IMPORT `soga-part2-formation`
IMPORT `soga-part3-effects`
IMPORT `soga-part4-performance`
IMPORT `soga-part5-unpaid-seller`
IMPORT `soga-part6-actions`
IMPORT `soga-part7-supplementary`
IMPORT `soga-interpretation`
IMPORT `soga-schedule`
IMPORT `soga-routes`

-- Sale of Goods Act 1979 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from 1/4/2022 -- THE SEAT LAYER.
--
-- SEAT: a buyer of goods under a contract of sale in Singapore, asking, on facts they can state, what terms are
-- implied, when the property and the risk pass, what they may do if the goods are late, defective or not as
-- described, and what they owe the seller.  This layer phrases the rules already encoded in the modules imported above as
-- the questions that person would ask.  Nothing here is new law.
--
-- PLAIN INPUTS.  Every question takes plain inputs -- yes/no, a number, a date, or a choice from a list -- one for
-- each fact the person can state, each with an @desc worded as the person would be asked.  The same module rule is
-- called with those inputs; no rule is restated here.
--   * Only the two gathered questions that take other provisions' answers, and the s 10(2) question about the terms of the contract, ask "Yes / No / I am not sure"
--     (`My answer`); "I am not sure" becomes NOTHING.
--   * An amount a question needs (a reasonable price, a market price, a loss) is a finding of fact the Act does not
--     compute; the person gives it.  The Act's own figures and tests are in the modules.
--   * The Act's borrowed terms (the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977, the Factors Act 1889, the general law of capacity,
--     bankruptcy, the common law) are inputs, as in the modules.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act itself gives; NOTHING means the Act does not
-- answer on these facts (see NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").  Never read NOTHING as no.
--
-- The Act is also an Act for the seller, the auctioneer, the carrier and the court.  Where a provision addresses only
-- one of them (ss 20A and 20B for co-owners, 41 to 43, 45 to 47 for the seller, 52 for the court), the rule is in
-- layer 1; the buyer meets it only through the questions below.

DECLARE `My answer` IS ONE OF
    Yes
    No
    `I am not sure`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Wiring: the person's answers become the modules' inputs.  Not questions.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- An answer of Yes, No or "I am not sure" as a MAYBE BOOLEAN.
GIVEN answer IS A `My answer`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`what I say, as a fact` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN Yes THEN JUST TRUE -- input mapping
    WHEN No THEN JUST FALSE -- input mapping
    WHEN `I am not sure` THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

-- The one provision (s 53(1)) whose answer to the rejection question is a no: a Yes means the Act says the buyer is not entitled.
GIVEN answer IS A `My answer`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`what I say, as the Act's no, if yes` MEANS
    CONSIDER answer
    WHEN Yes THEN JUST FALSE -- input mapping
    WHEN No THEN NOTHING -- input mapping
    WHEN `I am not sure` THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

-- A question worded the other way round from the rule: the Act's answer to the opposite question, turned round.  NOTHING stays NOTHING.
GIVEN rule IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`the Act's answer to the opposite question` MEANS
    CONSIDER rule
    WHEN JUST x THEN JUST (NOT x) -- input mapping
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- input mapping

-- A date that exists only if the thing has happened.
GIVEN happened IS A BOOLEAN
      onDate IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`my date if it has happened` MEANS
    IF happened THEN JUST onDate ELSE NOTHING -- input mapping

§ `The Sale of Goods Act 1979 -- my questions`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Does the Act apply to my contract, and in what words? (s 1)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 1(1).  The Act applies to a contract of sale of goods made on or after 1 January 1894 and does not apply to one made before.
@export Does the Sale of Goods Act apply to my contract (s 1(1))?
GIVEN `the date my contract was made` IS A DATE @desc On what date was your contract of sale made?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act apply to my contract` IS
    `s 1(1) this Act applies to the contract of sale of goods` `the date my contract was made`

-- s 1(2) to (4).  Nine sections carry a reference to the Schedule; for a contract made on a date the Schedule names, that section is modified.  A contract made before 1 January 1894 is outside the Schedule.  A date outside the Schedule's windows gets NOTHING: the section reads unmodified.
@export Does the Schedule change the wording of this section of the Act for my contract (s 1(2) to (4))?
GIVEN `the date my contract was made` IS A DATE @desc On what date was your contract of sale made?
      `the section` IS A `Section of the Act that refers to the Schedule` @desc Which section are you asking about?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Schedule change a section for my contract` IS
    `s 1(2) this Act applies to the contract subject to the modification of the section mentioned in the Schedule` `the date my contract was made` `the section`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `What kind of contract do I have? (ss 2 to 11)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 2(1).  A contract of sale of goods is a contract by which the seller transfers or agrees to transfer the property in goods to the buyer for a money consideration.
@export Is my contract a contract of sale of goods (s 2(1))?
GIVEN `the seller transfers or agrees to transfer the property in the goods to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the seller transfer, or agree to transfer, the property in goods to you?
      `it is for a money consideration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it for a money price?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my contract a contract of sale of goods` IS
    `s 2(1) the contract is a contract of sale of goods` `the seller transfers or agrees to transfer the property in the goods to me` `it is for a money consideration`

-- ss 2(4) to (6).  A contract is a sale when the property passes under it, an agreement to sell when it is to pass later or on a condition, and the agreement becomes a sale when the time elapses or the condition is fulfilled.
@export Is my contract a sale or an agreement to sell (ss 2(4) to (6))?
GIVEN `the property in the goods is transferred from the seller to me under the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the property in the goods been transferred to you under the contract?
      `the transfer of the property is to take place at a future time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the transfer of the property to take place at a future time?
      `the transfer of the property is subject to some condition later to be fulfilled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the transfer of the property subject to some condition that has still to be fulfilled?
      `the time has elapsed subject to which the property is to be transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that time elapsed?
      `the conditions have been fulfilled subject to which the property is to be transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have those conditions been fulfilled?
GIVETH A MAYBE `Stage of the contract`
DECIDE `Is my contract a sale or an agreement to sell` IS
    `ss 2(4) to (6) the stage of the contract of sale` `the property in the goods is transferred from the seller to me under the contract` `the transfer of the property is to take place at a future time` `the transfer of the property is subject to some condition later to be fulfilled` `the time has elapsed subject to which the property is to be transferred` `the conditions have been fulfilled subject to which the property is to be transferred`

-- ss 2(4) to (6), yes or no.  The question above names the stage; this one says whether any of the three rules applies at all.
@export Does one of the Act's rules naming the stage of my contract apply (ss 2(4) to (6))?
GIVEN `the property in the goods is transferred from the seller to me under the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the property in the goods been transferred to you under the contract?
      `the transfer of the property is to take place at a future time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the transfer of the property to take place at a future time?
      `the transfer of the property is subject to some condition later to be fulfilled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the transfer of the property subject to some condition that has still to be fulfilled?
      `the time has elapsed subject to which the property is to be transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has that time elapsed?
      `the conditions have been fulfilled subject to which the property is to be transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have those conditions been fulfilled?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does one of the rules naming the stage of my contract apply` IS
    `ss 2(4) to (6) a rule naming the stage of the contract applies` `the property in the goods is transferred from the seller to me under the contract` `the transfer of the property is to take place at a future time` `the transfer of the property is subject to some condition later to be fulfilled` `the time has elapsed subject to which the property is to be transferred` `the conditions have been fulfilled subject to which the property is to be transferred`

-- s 3(2).  Where necessaries are sold and delivered to a minor, or to a person incompetent to contract by reason of mental incapacity or drunkenness, he must pay a reasonable price for them.  The reasonable price is a finding of fact, so you give it.
@export What must I pay for necessaries sold and delivered to me (s 3(2))?
GIVEN `the goods are necessaries` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the goods suitable to your condition in life and your actual requirements at the time of the sale and delivery?
      `the goods were sold and delivered to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the goods sold and delivered to you?
      `I am a minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a minor?
      `I am incompetent to contract by reason of mental incapacity or drunkenness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you incompetent to contract by reason of mental incapacity or drunkenness?
      `a reasonable price for the goods` IS A NUMBER @desc What is a reasonable price for the goods, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What must I pay for necessaries` IS
    `s 3(2) the amount the buyer must pay for necessaries sold and delivered` `the goods are necessaries` `the goods were sold and delivered to me` `I am a minor` `I am incompetent to contract by reason of mental incapacity or drunkenness` `a reasonable price for the goods`

-- s 3(2), yes or no.  The question above gives the amount; this one says whether the duty applies at all.
@export Must I pay a reasonable price for necessaries sold and delivered to me (s 3(2))?
GIVEN `the goods are necessaries` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the goods suitable to your condition in life and your actual requirements at the time of the sale and delivery?
      `the goods were sold and delivered to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the goods sold and delivered to you?
      `I am a minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a minor?
      `I am incompetent to contract by reason of mental incapacity or drunkenness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you incompetent to contract by reason of mental incapacity or drunkenness?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I pay a reasonable price for necessaries` IS
    `s 3(2) the duty to pay a reasonable price for necessaries applies` `the goods are necessaries` `the goods were sold and delivered to me` `I am a minor` `I am incompetent to contract by reason of mental incapacity or drunkenness`

-- s 4(1).  A contract of sale may be made in writing, by word of mouth, partly both, or implied from conduct, subject to this and any other Act.
@export May my contract of sale be made in this form (s 4(1))?
GIVEN `the form of my contract` IS A `Form of the contract of sale` @desc In what form was your contract made?
      `another enactment requires a particular form or formality` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does another Act require a particular form or formality for this contract?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my contract be made in this form` IS
    `s 4(1) a contract of sale may be made in this form` `the form of my contract` `another enactment requires a particular form or formality`

-- s 6.  A contract for the sale of specific goods is void if the goods had perished when it was made without the seller knowing.
@export Is my contract void because the goods had already perished (s 6)?
GIVEN `the contract is for the sale of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of specific goods?
      `the goods had perished when the contract was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had the goods perished when the contract was made?
      `the seller did not know that the goods had perished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller not know that the goods had perished?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my contract void because the goods had perished` IS
    `s 6 the contract is void` `the contract is for the sale of specific goods` `the goods had perished when the contract was made` `the seller did not know that the goods had perished`

-- s 7.  An agreement to sell specific goods is avoided if the goods perish, with no fault of either party, before the risk passes to the buyer.
@export Is my agreement to buy avoided because the goods perished (s 7)?
GIVEN `there is an agreement to sell specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there an agreement to sell specific goods?
      `the goods perished after the agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the goods perish after the agreement was made?
      `there was no fault on the part of the seller or me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was there no fault on the part of the seller or of you?
      `the goods perished before the risk passed to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the goods perish before the risk passed to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my agreement avoided because the goods perished` IS
    `s 7 the agreement to sell is avoided` `there is an agreement to sell specific goods` `the goods perished after the agreement` `there was no fault on the part of the seller or me` `the goods perished before the risk passed to me`

-- s 8(1).  The price may be fixed by the contract, left to be fixed in an agreed manner, or determined by the course of dealing.  If it is none of these, s 8(2) answers.
@export Was the price of my contract determined in one of the ways the Act mentions (s 8(1))?
GIVEN `the price is fixed by the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract fix the price?
      `the price is left to be fixed in a manner agreed by the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract leave the price to be fixed in a way it agrees?
      `the price is determined by the course of dealing between the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the price determined by the course of dealing between you and the seller?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was the price determined in one of the ways s 8(1) mentions` IS
    `s 8(1) the price is determined as the subsection mentions` `the price is fixed by the contract` `the price is left to be fixed in a manner agreed by the contract` `the price is determined by the course of dealing between the parties`

-- s 8(2).  Where the price is not determined in any of the three ways in s 8(1), the buyer must pay a reasonable price.  What is reasonable is a question of fact, so you give it.
@export What must I pay when the contract did not fix the price (s 8(2))?
GIVEN `the price is fixed by the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract fix the price?
      `the price is left to be fixed in a manner agreed by the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract leave the price to be fixed in a way it agrees?
      `the price is determined by the course of dealing between the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the price determined by the course of dealing between you and the seller?
      `a reasonable price on the circumstances of the case` IS A NUMBER @desc What is a reasonable price, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What must I pay when the price was not fixed` IS
    `s 8(2) the amount the buyer must pay where the price is not determined` `the price is fixed by the contract` `the price is left to be fixed in a manner agreed by the contract` `the price is determined by the course of dealing between the parties` `a reasonable price on the circumstances of the case`

-- s 8(2), yes or no.  The question above gives the amount; this one says whether the duty applies at all.
@export Must I pay a reasonable price because the price was not determined (s 8(2))?
GIVEN `the price is fixed by the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract fix the price?
      `the price is left to be fixed in a manner agreed by the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract leave the price to be fixed in a way it agrees?
      `the price is determined by the course of dealing between the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the price determined by the course of dealing between you and the seller?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I pay a reasonable price because the price was not determined` IS
    `s 8(2) the duty to pay a reasonable price applies` `the price is fixed by the contract` `the price is left to be fixed in a manner agreed by the contract` `the price is determined by the course of dealing between the parties`

-- s 10(1).  Unless a different intention appears from the terms of the contract, a stipulation about the time of payment is not of the essence.
@export Is the time for payment of the essence of my contract (s 10(1))?
GIVEN `a different intention appears from the terms of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the terms of the contract show a different intention about the time of payment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the time for payment of the essence` IS
    `s 10(1) a stipulation as to time of payment is of the essence of the contract` `a different intention appears from the terms of the contract`

-- s 10(2).  Whether any other stipulation as to time (for example the time of delivery) is of the essence depends on the terms of the contract.  The terms are outside the text, so you give them.
@export Is time for any other stipulation, such as delivery, of the essence of my contract (s 10(2))?
GIVEN `the terms of the contract make the stipulation as to time of the essence` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the terms of your contract make the stipulation about time (for example, the time of delivery) of the essence?  Answer Yes, No or I am not sure.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is time for another stipulation of the essence` IS
    `s 10(2) any other stipulation as to time is of the essence of the contract` (`what I say, as a fact` `the terms of the contract make the stipulation as to time of the essence`)

-- s 11(2).  Whether a stipulation is a condition or a warranty depends on the construction of the contract; a stipulation may be a condition though called a warranty.  The construction is outside the text, so you give it.
@export Is this stipulation in my contract a condition or a warranty (s 11(2))?
GIVEN `what the contract calls the stipulation` IS A `Label the contract gives a stipulation` @desc What does the contract call the stipulation?
      `how the contract is construed` IS A `Construction of a stipulation` @desc How is the stipulation construed?
GIVETH A MAYBE `Kind of stipulation`
DECIDE `Is this stipulation a condition or a warranty` IS
    `s 11(2) the stipulation is a condition or a warranty` `what the contract calls the stipulation` `how the contract is construed`

-- s 11(3).  After the buyer has accepted the goods or part of them, in a contract that is not severable, a breach of condition can only be treated as a breach of warranty, unless the contract says otherwise; section 35A can preserve the right to reject.
@export Having accepted the goods, can I only treat the seller's breach of condition as a breach of warranty (s 11(3))?
GIVEN `the contract of sale is severable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract severable?
      `I have accepted the goods or part of them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you accepted the goods or part of them?
      `an express or implied term of the contract allows rejection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does an express or implied term of the contract allow you to reject the goods?
      `section 35A preserves my right to reject` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 35A (partial acceptance) preserve your right to reject the rest?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I only treat the breach as a breach of warranty after accepting` IS
    `s 11(3) the breach of a condition can only be treated as a breach of warranty` `the contract of sale is severable` `I have accepted the goods or part of them` `an express or implied term of the contract allows rejection` `section 35A preserves my right to reject`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `What terms does the Act put into my contract? (ss 12 to 15A)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 12(1).  There is an implied condition that the seller has a right to sell (in a sale), or will have one when the property is to pass (in an agreement to sell), unless s 12(3) applies.
@export Has the seller broken the implied condition that he has a right to sell (s 12(1))?
GIVEN `it appears from the contract that the seller was to transfer only such title as he or a third person may have` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it appear from the contract that the seller was to transfer only such title as he or a third person may have?
      `such an intention is to be inferred from the circumstances of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is such an intention to be inferred from the circumstances of the contract?
      `the contract is a sale` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract a sale (rather than an agreement to sell)?
      `the seller has a right to sell the goods when the contract is made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller have a right to sell the goods when the contract was made?
      `the seller will have a right to sell the goods when the property is to pass` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the seller have a right to sell the goods when the property is to pass?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the seller broken the implied condition about his right to sell` IS
    `s 12(1) the implied condition as to the right to sell is broken` (`s 12(3) this subsection applies to the contract of sale` `it appears from the contract that the seller was to transfer only such title as he or a third person may have` `such an intention is to be inferred from the circumstances of the contract` EQUALS JUST TRUE) `the contract is a sale` `the seller has a right to sell the goods when the contract is made` `the seller will have a right to sell the goods when the property is to pass`

-- s 12(2)(a).  There is an implied warranty that the goods are free, and will remain free until the property passes, from any charge or encumbrance not disclosed or known to the buyer before the contract was made.
@export Has the seller broken the implied warranty that the goods are free from an undisclosed charge (s 12(2)(a))?
GIVEN `it appears from the contract that the seller was to transfer only such title as he or a third person may have` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does it appear from the contract that the seller was to transfer only such title as he or a third person may have?
      `such an intention is to be inferred from the circumstances of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is such an intention to be inferred from the circumstances of the contract?
      `the goods are subject to a charge or encumbrance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the goods subject to a charge or encumbrance?
      `it was disclosed or known to me before the contract was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was it disclosed, or known to you, before the contract was made?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the seller broken the implied warranty about charges` IS
    `s 12(2)(a) the implied warranty that the goods are free from an undisclosed charge or encumbrance is broken` (`s 12(3) this subsection applies to the contract of sale` `it appears from the contract that the seller was to transfer only such title as he or a third person may have` `such an intention is to be inferred from the circumstances of the contract` EQUALS JUST TRUE) `the goods are subject to a charge or encumbrance` `it was disclosed or known to me before the contract was made`

-- s 13(1).  Where goods are sold by description there is an implied condition that they correspond with the description.
@export Do the goods I received match their description (s 13(1))?
GIVEN `the contract is for the sale of goods by description` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the goods sold by description?
      `the goods correspond with the description` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the goods correspond with the description?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the seller broken the implied condition about description` IS
    `s 13(1) the implied condition that the goods will correspond with the description is broken` `the contract is for the sale of goods by description` `the goods correspond with the description`

-- s 14(1).  Except as ss 14 and 15 provide, and subject to any other enactment, there is no implied condition or warranty about the quality or fitness of goods supplied under a contract of sale.  A private seller therefore implies none.
@export Does the Act imply any condition or warranty about the quality or fitness of the goods (s 14(1))?
GIVEN `the seller sells the goods in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller sell the goods in the course of a business?
      `usage annexes an implied condition or warranty about quality or fitness for a particular purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a usage of trade annex an implied condition or warranty about quality or fitness for a particular purpose to your contract?
      `the contract is for sale by sample` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract a sale by sample?
      `another enactment provides an implied condition or warranty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does another Act provide an implied condition or warranty about the quality or fitness of the goods?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act imply a term about quality or fitness` IS
    `s 14(1) there is an implied condition or warranty about the quality or fitness of the goods` (`the seller sells the goods in the course of a business` OR `usage annexes an implied condition or warranty about quality or fitness for a particular purpose`) `the contract is for sale by sample` `another enactment provides an implied condition or warranty`

-- s 14(2), (2A).  Where the seller sells in the course of a business there is an implied condition of satisfactory quality, which is the standard a reasonable person would regard as satisfactory; it does not extend to a matter specifically drawn to the buyer's attention, or one his examination ought to have revealed.
@export Are the goods of satisfactory quality, or has the seller broken the implied condition (s 14(2))?
GIVEN `the seller sells the goods in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller sell the goods in the course of a business?
      `a reasonable person would regard the goods as satisfactory, taking account of any description of the goods, the price (if relevant) and all the other relevant circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would a reasonable person regard the goods as satisfactory, taking account of any description of the goods, the price and all the other circumstances?
      `the matter making the quality unsatisfactory was drawn to my attention, or my examination before the contract ought to have revealed it, or (sale by sample) it would have been apparent on reasonable examination of the sample` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the matter that makes the quality unsatisfactory specifically drawn to your attention before the contract, or would your examination before the contract have revealed it, or (if you bought by sample) would a reasonable examination of the sample have shown it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the seller broken the implied condition of satisfactory quality` IS
    `s 14(2) the implied condition of satisfactory quality is broken` `the seller sells the goods in the course of a business` (`s 14(2A) the goods are of satisfactory quality` `a reasonable person would regard the goods as satisfactory, taking account of any description of the goods, the price (if relevant) and all the other relevant circumstances` EQUALS JUST TRUE) `the matter making the quality unsatisfactory was drawn to my attention, or my examination before the contract ought to have revealed it, or (sale by sample) it would have been apparent on reasonable examination of the sample`

-- s 14(3).  Where the seller sells in the course of a business and the buyer makes a particular purpose known, there is an implied condition that the goods are reasonably fit for it, unless the buyer does not rely, or it is unreasonable for him to rely, on the seller's skill or judgment.
@export Are the goods reasonably fit for the purpose I made known, or has the seller broken the implied condition (s 14(3))?
GIVEN `the seller sells the goods in the course of a business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller sell the goods in the course of a business?
      `I expressly or by implication made a particular purpose known to the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you, expressly or by implication, make a particular purpose known to the seller?
      `the price is payable by instalments and the goods were previously sold by a credit-broker to the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the price payable by instalments, and were the goods previously sold by a credit-broker to the seller?
      `I expressly or by implication made a particular purpose known to the credit-broker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you, expressly or by implication, make a particular purpose known to the credit-broker?
      `the circumstances show that I do not rely on the skill or judgment of the seller or credit-broker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the circumstances show that you do not rely on the skill or judgment of the seller or credit-broker?
      `the circumstances show that it is unreasonable for me to rely on the skill or judgment of the seller or credit-broker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the circumstances show that it is unreasonable for you to rely on the skill or judgment of the seller or credit-broker?
      `the goods are reasonably fit for that purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the goods reasonably fit for that purpose?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the seller broken the implied condition of fitness for my purpose` IS
    `s 14(3) the implied condition of reasonable fitness is broken` (`s 14(3) there is an implied condition that the goods are reasonably fit for the purpose made known` `the seller sells the goods in the course of a business` `I expressly or by implication made a particular purpose known to the seller` `the price is payable by instalments and the goods were previously sold by a credit-broker to the seller` `I expressly or by implication made a particular purpose known to the credit-broker` `the circumstances show that I do not rely on the skill or judgment of the seller or credit-broker` `the circumstances show that it is unreasonable for me to rely on the skill or judgment of the seller or credit-broker` EQUALS JUST TRUE) `the goods are reasonably fit for that purpose`

-- s 15(2).  In a sale by sample there are implied conditions that the bulk will correspond with the sample in quality and be free from a defect making the quality unsatisfactory that a reasonable examination of the sample would not reveal.
@export Does the bulk I received match the sample (s 15(2)(a))?
GIVEN `the contract is for sale by sample` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sale by sample?
      `the bulk corresponds with the sample in quality` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the bulk correspond with the sample in quality?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the seller broken the implied condition about the sample` IS
    `s 15(2)(a) the implied condition that the bulk will correspond with the sample in quality is broken` `the contract is for sale by sample` `the bulk corresponds with the sample in quality`

-- s 15(2)(b).
@export Do the goods have a defect that a reasonable examination of the sample would not have revealed (s 15(2)(b))?
GIVEN `the contract is for sale by sample` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sale by sample?
      `the goods have a defect making their quality unsatisfactory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the goods have a defect making their quality unsatisfactory?
      `the defect would not be apparent on reasonable examination of the sample` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the defect not have been apparent on a reasonable examination of the sample?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the seller broken the implied condition about hidden defects in a sale by sample` IS
    `s 15(2)(b) the implied condition that the goods will be free from a defect not apparent on examination of the sample is broken` `the contract is for sale by sample` `the goods have a defect making their quality unsatisfactory` `the defect would not be apparent on reasonable examination of the sample`

-- s 15A(1).  A buyer who is not a consumer may not reject for a breach of an implied condition (ss 13 to 15) so slight that rejection would be unreasonable; the breach is treated as a breach of warranty.
@export Is the seller's breach so slight that I may only claim damages (s 15A(1))?
GIVEN `I would, apart from this rule, have the right to reject the goods by reason of the seller's breach of a condition implied by section 13, 14 or 15` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would you have the right to reject the goods because the seller broke a condition implied by section 13, 14 or 15?
      `the breach is so slight that it would be unreasonable for me to reject the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the breach so slight that it would be unreasonable for you to reject the goods?
      `I deal as consumer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you deal as consumer?
      `a contrary intention appears in, or is to be implied from, the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a contrary intention appear in, or can one be implied from, the contract?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the breach so slight that I may only claim damages` IS
    `s 15A(1) the breach is not treated as a breach of condition but may be treated as a breach of warranty` `I would, apart from this rule, have the right to reject the goods by reason of the seller's breach of a condition implied by section 13, 14 or 15` `the breach is so slight that it would be unreasonable for me to reject the goods` `I deal as consumer` `a contrary intention appears in, or is to be implied from, the contract`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `When do I become the owner of the goods? (ss 16 to 19)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 16.  Where the goods are unascertained, no property is transferred to the buyer unless and until they are ascertained (subject to s 20A).
@export Has the property in unascertained goods passed to me (s 16)?
GIVEN `the contract is for the sale of unascertained goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of unascertained goods?
      `the goods are ascertained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the goods been ascertained?
      `section 20A applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 20A (a share in a bulk you have paid for) apply?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the property in unascertained goods passed to me` IS
    `s 16 property in unascertained goods is transferred to the buyer` `the contract is for the sale of unascertained goods` `the goods are ascertained` `section 20A applies`

-- s 16, the bar.  The question above asks whether the property has passed; this one asks whether it is still barred from passing.  While the goods are unascertained the answer is Yes; once they are ascertained it is No, and s 16 itself does not say that the property has passed.
@export Is the property in unascertained goods still barred from passing to me (s 16)?
GIVEN `the contract is for the sale of unascertained goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of unascertained goods?
      `the goods are ascertained` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the goods been ascertained?
      `section 20A applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does section 20A (a share in a bulk you have paid for) apply?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the property in unascertained goods still barred from passing to me` IS
    `s 16 property in unascertained goods cannot yet be transferred` `the contract is for the sale of unascertained goods` `the goods are ascertained` `section 20A applies`

-- s 18 Rule 1.  In an unconditional contract for specific goods in a deliverable state, the property passes when the contract is made, though payment or delivery is postponed.
@export Has the property in specific goods in a deliverable state passed to me (s 18, Rule 1)?
GIVEN `a different intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a different intention appear from the terms of the contract, the conduct of the parties and the circumstances?
      `the contract is an unconditional contract for the sale of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it an unconditional contract for the sale of specific goods?
      `the goods are in a deliverable state` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the goods in a deliverable state?
      `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the contract made?
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For which date are you asking whether the property has passed?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the property in the specific goods passed to me under Rule 1` IS
    `s 18(a) Rule 1: the property in the goods has passed to the buyer` `a different intention appears` `the contract is an unconditional contract for the sale of specific goods` `the goods are in a deliverable state` `the date the contract was made` `the date I am asking about`

-- s 18 Rule 2.  Where the seller is bound to do something to put specific goods into a deliverable state, the property does not pass until it is done and the buyer has notice.
@export Has the property passed, where the seller had to put the goods into a deliverable state (s 18, Rule 2)?
GIVEN `a different intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a different intention appear from the terms of the contract, the conduct of the parties and the circumstances?
      `the contract is for the sale of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of specific goods?
      `the seller is bound to do something to the goods to put them into a deliverable state` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller bound to do something to the goods to put them into a deliverable state?
      `the seller has done it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller done it?
      `the date the seller did it` IS A DATE @desc On what date did the seller do it? (any date if not yet)
      `I have had notice that it was done` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had notice that it was done?
      `the date I had notice` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you have notice? (any date if not yet)
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For which date are you asking?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the property passed under Rule 2` IS
    `s 18(b) Rule 2: the property in the goods has passed to the buyer` `a different intention appears` `the contract is for the sale of specific goods` `the seller is bound to do something to the goods to put them into a deliverable state` (`my date if it has happened` `the seller has done it` `the date the seller did it`) (`my date if it has happened` `I have had notice that it was done` `the date I had notice`) `the date I am asking about`

-- s 18 Rule 2, the bar.  The question above asks whether the property has passed; this one asks whether it is still barred from passing.  Before the thing is done and you have notice the answer is Yes; from then on it is No, and the Act still does not say that the property has passed.
@export Is the property still barred from passing to me, where the seller had to put the goods into a deliverable state (s 18, Rule 2)?
GIVEN `a different intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a different intention appear from the terms of the contract, the conduct of the parties and the circumstances?
      `the contract is for the sale of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of specific goods?
      `the seller is bound to do something to the goods to put them into a deliverable state` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller bound to do something to the goods to put them into a deliverable state?
      `the seller has done it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller done it?
      `the date the seller did it` IS A DATE @desc On what date did the seller do it? (any date if not yet)
      `I have had notice that it was done` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had notice that it was done?
      `the date I had notice` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you have notice? (any date if not yet)
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For which date are you asking?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the property still barred from passing under Rule 2` IS
    `s 18(b) Rule 2: the property cannot yet pass` `a different intention appears` `the contract is for the sale of specific goods` `the seller is bound to do something to the goods to put them into a deliverable state` (`my date if it has happened` `the seller has done it` `the date the seller did it`) (`my date if it has happened` `I have had notice that it was done` `the date I had notice`) `the date I am asking about`

-- s 18 Rule 3.  Where the seller must weigh, measure or test specific goods in a deliverable state to ascertain the price, the property does not pass until it is done and the buyer has notice.
@export Has the property passed, where the seller had to weigh or measure the goods to find the price (s 18, Rule 3)?
GIVEN `a different intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a different intention appear from the terms of the contract, the conduct of the parties and the circumstances?
      `the contract is for the sale of specific goods in a deliverable state` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of specific goods in a deliverable state?
      `the seller is bound to weigh, measure, test or do some other act or thing with reference to the goods for the purpose of ascertaining the price` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller bound to weigh, measure, test or do some other act or thing with reference to the goods to ascertain the price?
      `the seller has done it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller done it?
      `the date the seller did it` IS A DATE @desc On what date did the seller do it? (any date if not yet)
      `I have had notice that it was done` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had notice that it was done?
      `the date I had notice` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you have notice? (any date if not yet)
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For which date are you asking?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the property passed under Rule 3` IS
    `s 18(c) Rule 3: the property in the goods has passed to the buyer` `a different intention appears` `the contract is for the sale of specific goods in a deliverable state` `the seller is bound to weigh, measure, test or do some other act or thing with reference to the goods for the purpose of ascertaining the price` (`my date if it has happened` `the seller has done it` `the date the seller did it`) (`my date if it has happened` `I have had notice that it was done` `the date I had notice`) `the date I am asking about`

-- s 18 Rule 3, the bar.  The question above asks whether the property has passed; this one asks whether it is still barred from passing.  Before the act is done and you have notice the answer is Yes; from then on it is No, and the Act still does not say that the property has passed.
@export Is the property still barred from passing to me, where the seller had to weigh or measure the goods to find the price (s 18, Rule 3)?
GIVEN `a different intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a different intention appear from the terms of the contract, the conduct of the parties and the circumstances?
      `the contract is for the sale of specific goods in a deliverable state` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of specific goods in a deliverable state?
      `the seller is bound to weigh, measure, test or do some other act or thing with reference to the goods for the purpose of ascertaining the price` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller bound to weigh, measure, test or do some other act or thing with reference to the goods to ascertain the price?
      `the seller has done it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller done it?
      `the date the seller did it` IS A DATE @desc On what date did the seller do it? (any date if not yet)
      `I have had notice that it was done` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had notice that it was done?
      `the date I had notice` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you have notice? (any date if not yet)
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For which date are you asking?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the property still barred from passing under Rule 3` IS
    `s 18(c) Rule 3: the property cannot yet pass` `a different intention appears` `the contract is for the sale of specific goods in a deliverable state` `the seller is bound to weigh, measure, test or do some other act or thing with reference to the goods for the purpose of ascertaining the price` (`my date if it has happened` `the seller has done it` `the date the seller did it`) (`my date if it has happened` `I have had notice that it was done` `the date I had notice`) `the date I am asking about`

-- s 18 Rule 4.  Goods delivered on approval or on sale or return: the property passes when the buyer signifies approval, or adopts the transaction, or keeps the goods without giving notice of rejection beyond the time fixed or, if none, a reasonable time.
@export Has the property passed, where I have the goods on approval or on sale or return (s 18, Rule 4)?
GIVEN `a different intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a different intention appear from the terms of the contract, the conduct of the parties and the circumstances?
      `the goods were delivered to me on approval or on sale or return` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the goods delivered to you on approval or on sale or return?
      `I have signified approval or acceptance to the seller or done any other act adopting the transaction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you signified your approval or acceptance to the seller, or done any other act adopting the transaction?
      `the date I did so` IS A DATE @desc On what date did you do so? (any date if not yet)
      `I retained the goods without giving notice of rejection` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you keep the goods without giving notice of rejection?
      `a time has been fixed for the return of the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a time been fixed for the return of the goods?
      `the date the time fixed for the return of the goods expires` IS A DATE @desc On what date does the time fixed for the return of the goods expire? (any date if none)
      `the date a reasonable time for the return of the goods expires` IS A DATE @desc On what date does a reasonable time for the return of the goods expire?
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For which date are you asking?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the property passed under Rule 4` IS
    `s 18(d) Rule 4: the property in the goods has passed to the buyer` `a different intention appears` `the goods were delivered to me on approval or on sale or return` (`my date if it has happened` `I have signified approval or acceptance to the seller or done any other act adopting the transaction` `the date I did so`) `I retained the goods without giving notice of rejection` `a time has been fixed for the return of the goods` `the date the time fixed for the return of the goods expires` `the date a reasonable time for the return of the goods expires` `the date I am asking about`

-- s 18 Rule 5(1).  Unascertained or future goods sold by description: the property passes when goods of that description in a deliverable state are unconditionally appropriated to the contract by one party with the assent of the other.
@export Has the property passed in unascertained or future goods once they were appropriated to my contract (s 18, Rule 5(1))?
GIVEN `a different intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a different intention appear from the terms of the contract, the conduct of the parties and the circumstances?
      `the contract is for the sale of unascertained or future goods by description` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of unascertained or future goods by description?
      `goods of that description and in a deliverable state are unconditionally appropriated to the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have goods of that description, in a deliverable state, been unconditionally appropriated to the contract?
      `the appropriation is by the seller with the assent of the buyer, or by the buyer with the assent of the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the appropriation made by the seller with your assent, or by you with the seller's assent?
      `the date of the appropriation` IS A DATE @desc On what date was the appropriation made?
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc For which date are you asking?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has the property passed under Rule 5(1)` IS
    `s 18(e)(i) Rule 5(i): the property in the goods has passed to the buyer` `a different intention appears` `the contract is for the sale of unascertained or future goods by description` `goods of that description and in a deliverable state are unconditionally appropriated to the contract` `the appropriation is by the seller with the assent of the buyer, or by the buyer with the assent of the seller` `the date of the appropriation` `the date I am asking about`

-- s 19(1).  Where the seller reserves the right of disposal, the property does not pass until the conditions he imposes are fulfilled, even though the goods are delivered.
-- Answered through the bar (ruled 2026-11-05, "until" bars): Yes until the conditions are fulfilled, No from then on or where nothing was reserved.  Until the second revision of 2026-11-05 this question called the "property passes" function directly, so it answered No while the seller kept the property: that was the question worded the wrong way round, and is fixed here.
@export Does the seller keep the property because he reserved the right of disposal (s 19(1))?
GIVEN `the contract is for the sale of specific goods or the goods are subsequently appropriated to the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for specific goods, or have the goods been appropriated to it?
      `the seller has reserved the right of disposal by the terms of the contract or appropriation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller reserved the right of disposal in the contract or on appropriation?
      `the conditions imposed by the seller are fulfilled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the conditions the seller imposed been fulfilled?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the seller keep the property because he reserved the right of disposal` IS
    `s 19(1) the property cannot yet pass because the right of disposal is reserved` `the contract is for the sale of specific goods or the goods are subsequently appropriated to the contract` `the seller has reserved the right of disposal by the terms of the contract or appropriation` `the conditions imposed by the seller are fulfilled`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Who bears the risk of loss? (s 20)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 20(1).  Unless otherwise agreed, the goods remain at the seller's risk until the property is transferred to the buyer, and then at the buyer's risk, whether delivery has been made or not.
@export Who bears the risk if the goods are lost or damaged (s 20(1))?
GIVEN `it is otherwise agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you and the seller agreed otherwise about who bears the risk?
      `the property in the goods has been transferred to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the property in the goods been transferred to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE `Party to the sale`
DECIDE `Who bears the risk of loss` IS
    `s 20(1) the party at whose risk the goods are` `it is otherwise agreed` `the property in the goods has been transferred to me`

-- s 20(2).  Where delivery has been delayed through the fault of either party, the goods are at the risk of the party at fault as regards any loss which might not have occurred but for the fault.
@export Who bears the loss when delivery was delayed by someone's fault (s 20(2))?
GIVEN `delivery has been delayed through my fault` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was delivery delayed through your fault?
      `delivery has been delayed through the fault of the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was delivery delayed through the seller's fault?
      `the loss might not have occurred but for such fault` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Might the loss not have occurred but for the fault?
GIVETH A MAYBE `Party to the sale`
DECIDE `Who bears the loss when delivery was delayed by fault` IS
    `s 20(2) the party at whose risk the goods are as regards the loss` `delivery has been delayed through my fault` `delivery has been delayed through the fault of the seller` `the loss might not have occurred but for such fault`

-- s 20(2), yes or no.  The question above names the party; this one says whether the rule applies at all.
@export Does the rule on delay through someone's fault apply (s 20(2))?
GIVEN `delivery has been delayed through my fault` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was delivery delayed through your fault?
      `delivery has been delayed through the fault of the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was delivery delayed through the seller's fault?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the rule on delay through fault apply` IS
    `s 20(2) the rule on delay through the fault of a party applies` `delivery has been delayed through my fault` `delivery has been delayed through the fault of the seller`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Buying part of a bulk (ss 20A and 20B)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 20A(1).  A buyer who has paid the price for part of an identified bulk of unascertained goods becomes an owner in common of the bulk.
@export Do I own a share of the bulk I have paid for (s 20A(1))?
GIVEN `the contract is for the sale of a specified quantity of unascertained goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for a specified quantity of unascertained goods?
      `the goods or some of them form part of a bulk identified in the contract or by subsequent agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do the goods form part of a bulk identified in the contract or by later agreement?
      `I have paid the price for some or all of the goods which are the subject of the contract and which form part of the bulk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you paid the price for some or all of those goods?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I own a share of the bulk` IS
    `s 20A(1) section 20A applies to the contract` `the contract is for the sale of a specified quantity of unascertained goods` `the goods or some of them form part of a bulk identified in the contract or by subsequent agreement` `I have paid the price for some or all of the goods which are the subject of the contract and which form part of the bulk`

-- s 20A(3).  The undivided share is the quantity of goods paid for and due to the buyer out of the bulk, divided by the quantity in the bulk at that time.
@export What undivided share of the bulk is mine (s 20A(3))?
GIVEN `the quantity of goods paid for and due to me out of the bulk` IS A NUMBER @desc What quantity of goods have you paid for and are due to you out of the bulk?
      `the quantity of goods in the bulk at that time` IS A NUMBER @desc What quantity of goods is in the bulk at that time?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What undivided share of the bulk is mine` IS
    `s 20A(3) the undivided share of the buyer in the bulk` `the quantity of goods paid for and due to me out of the bulk` `the quantity of goods in the bulk at that time`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Buying goods from someone who may not own them (ss 21 to 25)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 21(1).  Where goods are sold by a person who is not their owner and without the owner's authority or consent, the buyer acquires no better title than the seller had, unless the owner is by conduct precluded from denying the seller's authority.
@export Do I get a better title than the seller had (s 21(1))?
GIVEN `the goods are sold by a person who is not their owner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did a person who is not the owner sell you the goods?
      `he sells under the authority or with the consent of the owner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he sell under the authority or with the consent of the owner?
      `the owner is by his conduct precluded from denying the seller's authority to sell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the owner, by his conduct, precluded from denying the seller's authority to sell?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I get a better title than the seller had` IS
    `s 21(1) the buyer acquires a better title to the goods than the seller had` `the goods are sold by a person who is not their owner` `he sells under the authority or with the consent of the owner` `the owner is by his conduct precluded from denying the seller's authority to sell`

-- s 23.  Where the seller's title is voidable but has not been avoided at the time of the sale, the buyer acquires a good title if he buys in good faith and without notice of the seller's defect of title.
@export Do I get a good title from a seller whose own title was voidable (s 23)?
GIVEN `the seller of the goods has a voidable title to them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller have a voidable title to the goods?
      `his title has not been avoided at the time of the sale` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had his title not been avoided at the time of the sale?
      `I buy the goods in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you buy the goods in good faith?
      `I have no notice of the seller's defect of title` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have no notice of the seller's defect of title?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I get a good title from a seller with a voidable title` IS
    `s 23 the buyer acquires a good title to the goods` `the seller of the goods has a voidable title to them` `his title has not been avoided at the time of the sale` `I buy the goods in good faith` `I have no notice of the seller's defect of title`

-- s 24.  Where a seller, still in possession of the goods or the documents of title, delivers or transfers them to a person who receives them in good faith and without notice of the previous sale, the delivery has the same effect as if the seller were expressly authorised by the owner.
@export Do I get a good title when the seller had already sold the goods to someone else but kept them (s 24)?
GIVEN `a person having sold goods continues or is in possession of the goods, or of the documents of title to the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller, having already sold the goods, keep possession of the goods or of the documents of title?
      `that person, or a mercantile agent acting for him, delivers or transfers the goods or documents of title to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he, or a mercantile agent acting for him, deliver or transfer the goods or documents of title to you?
      `I receive them in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you receive them in good faith?
      `I have no notice of the previous sale` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have no notice of the previous sale?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I get a good title when the seller had already sold the goods but kept them` IS
    `s 24 the delivery or transfer has the same effect as if the seller were expressly authorised by the owner to make it` `a person having sold goods continues or is in possession of the goods, or of the documents of title to the goods` `that person, or a mercantile agent acting for him, delivers or transfers the goods or documents of title to me` `I receive them in good faith` `I have no notice of the previous sale`

-- s 25.  Where a person who has bought or agreed to buy goods obtains possession with the seller's consent and delivers them to a person who receives in good faith and without notice of any lien or other right of the original seller, the delivery has the same effect as if made by a mercantile agent in possession with the owner's consent.
@export Do I get a good title from someone who has bought the goods but not yet paid the original seller (s 25)?
GIVEN `a person having bought or agreed to buy goods obtains, with the consent of the seller, possession of the goods or documents of title` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the person you bought from, having bought or agreed to buy the goods, obtain possession of them or of the documents of title with the original seller's consent?
      `that person, or a mercantile agent acting for him, delivers or transfers the goods or documents of title to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did he, or a mercantile agent acting for him, deliver or transfer the goods or documents of title to you?
      `I receive them in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you receive them in good faith?
      `I have no notice of any lien or other right of the original seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have no notice of any lien or other right of the original seller?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I get a good title from a buyer in possession` IS
    `s 25 the delivery or transfer has the same effect as if the person making it were a mercantile agent in possession with the consent of the owner` `a person having bought or agreed to buy goods obtains, with the consent of the seller, possession of the goods or documents of title` `that person, or a mercantile agent acting for him, delivers or transfers the goods or documents of title to me` `I receive them in good faith` `I have no notice of any lien or other right of the original seller`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Delivery, payment and the quantity delivered (ss 27 to 34)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 28.  Unless otherwise agreed, delivery and payment are concurrent conditions: the seller must be ready and willing to give possession in exchange for the price, and the buyer to pay in exchange for possession.
@export Must I pay when the goods are delivered, not before (s 28)?
GIVEN `it is otherwise agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you and the seller agreed otherwise about the order of delivery and payment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are delivery and payment concurrent` IS
    `s 28 delivery of the goods and payment of the price are concurrent conditions` `it is otherwise agreed`

-- s 29(2).  Apart from any contract, express or implied, the place of delivery is the seller's place of business or, if he has none, his residence; but specific goods known by the parties to be elsewhere when the contract was made are to be delivered there.  Where the contract fixes the place the Act does not answer, so this question answers NOTHING.
@export Where must the seller deliver the goods (s 29(2))?
GIVEN `the contract, express or implied, fixes the place of delivery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract, expressly or by implication, fix the place of delivery?
      `the seller has a place of business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the seller have a place of business?
      `the contract is for the sale of specific goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract for the sale of specific goods?
      `to the knowledge of the parties when the contract was made the goods were in some other place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did both of you know, when the contract was made, that the goods were in some other place?
GIVETH A MAYBE `Place of delivery`
DECIDE `Where must the goods be delivered` IS
    `s 29(2) the place of delivery` `the contract, express or implied, fixes the place of delivery` `the seller has a place of business` `the contract is for the sale of specific goods` `to the knowledge of the parties when the contract was made the goods were in some other place`

-- s 29(3).  Where the seller is bound to send the goods and no time is fixed, he must send them within a reasonable time.
@export Must the seller send the goods within a reasonable time (s 29(3))?
GIVEN `under the contract of sale the seller is bound to send the goods to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Under the contract, is the seller bound to send the goods to you?
      `a time for sending them is fixed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is a time for sending them fixed?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the seller send the goods within a reasonable time` IS
    `s 29(3) the seller is bound to send the goods within a reasonable time` `under the contract of sale the seller is bound to send the goods to me` `a time for sending them is fixed`

-- s 29(4).  Where the goods at the time of sale are in the possession of a third person, there is no delivery by the seller to the buyer unless and until the third person acknowledges to the buyer that he holds the goods on his behalf.  This asks whether delivery is still barred; the Act does not say that there is delivery once he has acknowledged.
@export Is delivery to me still not possible because a third person holds the goods (s 29(4))?
GIVEN `at the time of sale the goods are in the possession of a third person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc At the time of sale, were the goods in the possession of a third person?
      `the third person has acknowledged to the buyer that he holds the goods on his behalf` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the third person acknowledged to you that he holds the goods on your behalf?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is delivery still barred because a third person holds the goods` IS
    `s 29(4) delivery is not yet possible` `at the time of sale the goods are in the possession of a third person` `the third person has acknowledged to the buyer that he holds the goods on his behalf`

-- s 30(1).  Where the seller delivers less than he contracted to sell, the buyer may reject the goods delivered; if he accepts them he must pay for them at the contract rate.
@export May I reject goods delivered in a smaller quantity than I contracted for (s 30(1))?
GIVEN `the quantity delivered is less than the seller contracted to sell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the quantity delivered less than the seller contracted to sell?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I reject a short delivery` IS
    `s 30(1) the buyer may reject the goods delivered` `the quantity delivered is less than the seller contracted to sell`

-- s 30(1).  If the buyer accepts a short delivery he must pay at the contract rate.
@export What must I pay for a short delivery I accept (s 30(1))?
GIVEN `the quantity delivered is less than the seller contracted to sell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the quantity delivered less than the seller contracted to sell?
      `I accept the goods so delivered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you accept the goods so delivered?
      `the quantity delivered and accepted` IS A NUMBER @desc What quantity was delivered and accepted?
      `the contract rate per unit` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the contract rate per unit, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What must I pay for a short delivery I accept` IS
    `s 30(1) the amount the buyer must pay for the goods delivered and accepted` `the quantity delivered is less than the seller contracted to sell` `I accept the goods so delivered` `the quantity delivered and accepted` `the contract rate per unit`

-- s 30(1), yes or no.  The question above gives the amount; this one says whether the duty applies at all.
@export Must I pay at the contract rate for a short delivery that I accept (s 30(1))?
GIVEN `the quantity delivered is less than the seller contracted to sell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the quantity delivered less than the seller contracted to sell?
      `I accept the goods so delivered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you accept the goods so delivered?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I pay at the contract rate for a short delivery I accept` IS
    `s 30(1) the duty to pay at the contract rate for a short delivery that is accepted applies` `the quantity delivered is less than the seller contracted to sell` `I accept the goods so delivered`

-- s 30(2).  Where the seller delivers more than he contracted to sell, the buyer may accept the goods included in the contract and reject the rest, or reject the whole.
@export May I reject goods delivered in a larger quantity than I contracted for (s 30(2))?
GIVEN `the quantity delivered is larger than the seller contracted to sell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the quantity delivered larger than the seller contracted to sell?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I reject an excess delivery` IS
    `s 30(2) the buyer may reject the whole` `the quantity delivered is larger than the seller contracted to sell`

-- s 30(3).  If the buyer accepts the whole of an excess delivery he must pay for the whole at the contract rate.
@export What must I pay for an excess delivery that I accept in full (s 30(3))?
GIVEN `the quantity delivered is larger than the seller contracted to sell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the quantity delivered larger than the seller contracted to sell?
      `I accept the whole of the goods so delivered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you accept the whole of the goods delivered?
      `the whole quantity delivered` IS A NUMBER @desc What was the whole quantity delivered?
      `the contract rate per unit` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the contract rate per unit, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What must I pay for an excess delivery I accept in full` IS
    `s 30(3) the amount the buyer must pay for the whole of the goods delivered and accepted` `the quantity delivered is larger than the seller contracted to sell` `I accept the whole of the goods so delivered` `the whole quantity delivered` `the contract rate per unit`

-- s 30(3), yes or no.  The question above gives the amount; this one says whether the rule applies at all.
@export Must I pay at the contract rate for an excess delivery that I accept in full (s 30(3))?
GIVEN `the quantity delivered is larger than the seller contracted to sell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the quantity delivered larger than the seller contracted to sell?
      `I accept the whole of the goods so delivered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you accept the whole of the goods delivered?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I pay at the contract rate for an excess delivery I accept in full` IS
    `s 30(3) the duty to pay at the contract rate for the whole of an excess that is accepted applies` `the quantity delivered is larger than the seller contracted to sell` `I accept the whole of the goods so delivered`

-- s 30(2A).  A buyer who does not deal as consumer may not reject for a shortfall or excess so slight that it would be unreasonable to reject.
@export Am I barred from rejecting because the shortfall or excess is slight (s 30(2A))?
GIVEN `I deal as consumer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you deal as consumer?
      `the shortfall or excess is so slight that it would be unreasonable for me to reject` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the shortfall or excess so slight that it would be unreasonable for you to reject?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I barred from rejecting a slight shortfall or excess` IS
    `the Act's answer to the opposite question` (`s 30(2A) the buyer may reject the goods under subsection (1) or reject the whole under subsection (2)` `I deal as consumer` `the shortfall or excess is so slight that it would be unreasonable for me to reject`)

-- s 31(1).  Unless otherwise agreed, the buyer is not bound to accept delivery by instalments.
@export Must I accept delivery by instalments (s 31(1))?
GIVEN `it is otherwise agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you and the seller agreed that you must accept delivery by instalments?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I accept delivery by instalments` IS
    `s 31(1) the buyer is bound to accept delivery of the goods by instalments` `it is otherwise agreed`

-- s 32(1).  Delivery to a carrier for transmission to the buyer is prima facie delivery to the buyer.
@export Is delivery to the carrier delivery to me (s 32(1))?
GIVEN `in pursuance of the contract of sale the seller is authorised or required to send the goods to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller authorised or required, under the contract, to send the goods to you?
      `the seller delivers the goods to a carrier for the purpose of transmission to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller deliver the goods to a carrier for transmission to you?
      `the contrary is shown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there evidence that delivery to the carrier was not delivery to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is delivery to the carrier delivery to me` IS
    `s 32(1) delivery of the goods to the carrier is deemed to be a delivery of the goods to the buyer` `in pursuance of the contract of sale the seller is authorised or required to send the goods to me` `the seller delivers the goods to a carrier for the purpose of transmission to me` `the contrary is shown`

-- s 33.  Where the seller agrees to deliver the goods at his own risk at another place, the buyer must nevertheless (unless otherwise agreed) take any risk of deterioration necessarily incident to the course of transit.
@export Must I bear the risk of deterioration during transit where the seller delivers at his own risk (s 33)?
GIVEN `the seller agrees to deliver the goods at his own risk at a place other than where they are when sold` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller agree to deliver the goods at his own risk at a place other than where they were when sold?
      `it is otherwise agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you and the seller agreed otherwise?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I bear the risk of deterioration during transit` IS
    `s 33 the buyer must take any risk of deterioration in the goods necessarily incident to the course of transit` `the seller agrees to deliver the goods at his own risk at a place other than where they are when sold` `it is otherwise agreed`

-- s 34.  Unless otherwise agreed, when the seller tenders delivery he is bound on request to afford the buyer a reasonable opportunity of examining the goods.
@export Must the seller let me examine the goods (s 34)?
GIVEN `it is otherwise agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you and the seller agreed otherwise?
      `the seller tenders delivery of the goods to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller tendered delivery of the goods to you?
      `I request an opportunity of examining the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you asked for an opportunity of examining the goods?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the seller let me examine the goods` IS
    `s 34 the seller is bound to afford the buyer a reasonable opportunity of examining the goods` `it is otherwise agreed` `the seller tenders delivery of the goods to me` `I request an opportunity of examining the goods`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Acceptance, rejection and refusing delivery (ss 35 to 37)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- ss 35(1) to (7).  Gathered by hand: the buyer is treated as having accepted the goods on intimating acceptance or doing an act inconsistent with the seller's ownership (s 35(1)), but not until he has had a reasonable opportunity of examining them (s 35(2)); also by keeping them beyond a reasonable time without intimating rejection (s 35(4)) and by accepting any goods in a commercial unit (s 35(7)).
@export Am I treated as having accepted the goods (ss 35(1) to (7))?
GIVEN `I have told the seller that I have accepted the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you told the seller that you accept the goods?
      `the goods have been delivered to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have the goods been delivered to you?
      `I have done an act in relation to the goods which is inconsistent with the seller's ownership` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you done an act in relation to the goods that is inconsistent with the seller's ownership?
      `I have previously examined the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Had you examined the goods before they were delivered?
      `I have had a reasonable opportunity of examining the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you had a reasonable opportunity of examining the goods?
      `a reasonable time has lapsed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has a reasonable time passed?
      `I retain the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you still have the goods?
      `I have intimated to the seller that I have rejected them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you told the seller that you reject them?
      `the contract is for the sale of goods making one or more commercial units` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the contract cover goods making one or more commercial units (a unit that cannot be divided without materially impairing its value or character)?
      `I accept any goods included in a unit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you accepted any goods included in a unit?
      `the only thing I have done is to ask for, or agree to, a repair of the goods by or under an arrangement with the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the only thing you have done in relation to the goods that you asked for, or agreed to, their repair by or under an arrangement with the seller?
      `the only thing I have done is to deliver the goods to another under a sub-sale or other disposition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the only thing you have done in relation to the goods that you delivered them to another under a sub-sale or other disposition?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I treated as having accepted the goods` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the only thing I have done is to ask for, or agree to, a repair of the goods by or under an arrangement with the seller`
        THEN `s 35(6) the buyer is deemed to have accepted the goods by virtue of section 35 merely because of the reason` `he asks for, or agrees to, the repair of the goods by or under an arrangement with the seller`
        IF `the only thing I have done is to deliver the goods to another under a sub-sale or other disposition`
        THEN `s 35(6) the buyer is deemed to have accepted the goods by virtue of section 35 merely because of the reason` `the goods are delivered to another under a sub-sale or other disposition`
        OTHERWISE
            `ss 35(1) to (7) the buyer is deemed to have accepted the goods` (`s 35(1) the buyer is deemed to have accepted the goods under subsection (1)` `I have told the seller that I have accepted the goods` `the goods have been delivered to me` `I have done an act in relation to the goods which is inconsistent with the seller's ownership`) (`s 35(2) the buyer is deemed to have accepted the goods under subsection (1)` `the goods have been delivered to me` `I have previously examined the goods` `I have had a reasonable opportunity of examining the goods`) (`s 35(4) the buyer is deemed to have accepted the goods after a reasonable time` `a reasonable time has lapsed` `I retain the goods` `I have intimated to the seller that I have rejected them`) (`s 35(7) the buyer is deemed to have accepted all the goods making the unit` `the contract is for the sale of goods making one or more commercial units` `I accept any goods included in a unit`)

-- s 35A(1).  A buyer entitled to reject who accepts some of the goods does not by that lose the right to reject the rest, unless a contrary intention appears.
@export If I accept some of the goods, may I still reject the rest (s 35A(1))?
GIVEN `I have the right to reject the goods by reason of a breach on the part of the seller that affects some or all of them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you have the right to reject the goods because of a breach by the seller that affects some or all of them?
      `I accept some of the goods, including, where there are any goods unaffected by the breach, all such goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you accept some of the goods, including all the goods that the breach did not affect?
      `a contrary intention appears in, or is to be implied from, the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does a contrary intention appear in, or can one be implied from, the contract?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `If I accept some of the goods may I still reject the rest` IS
    `the Act's answer to the opposite question` (`s 35A(1) the buyer loses by accepting some of the goods his right to reject the rest` `I have the right to reject the goods by reason of a breach on the part of the seller that affects some or all of them` `I accept some of the goods, including, where there are any goods unaffected by the breach, all such goods` `a contrary intention appears in, or is to be implied from, the contract`)

-- s 36.  Unless otherwise agreed, a buyer who refuses goods delivered, having the right to do so, is not bound to return them to the seller; it is sufficient that he intimates his refusal.
@export Must I return goods I have rightly refused (s 36)?
GIVEN `it is otherwise agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you and the seller agreed otherwise?
      `goods are delivered to me and I refuse to accept them, having the right to do so` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the goods delivered to you, and do you refuse to accept them, having the right to do so?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I return goods I have rightly refused` IS
    `s 36 the buyer is bound to return the rejected goods to the seller` `it is otherwise agreed` `goods are delivered to me and I refuse to accept them, having the right to do so`

-- s 37(1).  Where the seller is ready and willing to deliver and the buyer does not take delivery within a reasonable time after request, the buyer is liable for the loss occasioned by his neglect or refusal and for a reasonable charge for the care and custody of the goods.
@export What do I owe the seller if I do not take delivery (s 37(1))?
GIVEN `the seller is ready and willing to deliver the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller ready and willing to deliver the goods?
      `the seller requests me to take delivery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller asked you to take delivery?
      `I do not within a reasonable time after the request take delivery of the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you failed to take delivery within a reasonable time after the request?
      `the loss occasioned by my neglect or refusal to take delivery` IS A NUMBER @desc What loss has the seller suffered through your neglect or refusal, in dollars?
      `a reasonable charge for the care and custody of the goods` IS A NUMBER @desc What is a reasonable charge for the care and custody of the goods, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `What do I owe the seller if I do not take delivery` IS
    `s 37(1) the amount the buyer is liable to the seller for` `the seller is ready and willing to deliver the goods` `the seller requests me to take delivery` `I do not within a reasonable time after the request take delivery of the goods` `the loss occasioned by my neglect or refusal to take delivery` `a reasonable charge for the care and custody of the goods`

-- s 37(1), yes or no.  The question above gives the amount; this one says whether the liability arises at all.
@export Am I liable to the seller for not taking delivery (s 37(1))?
GIVEN `the seller is ready and willing to deliver the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller ready and willing to deliver the goods?
      `the seller requests me to take delivery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller asked you to take delivery?
      `I do not within a reasonable time after the request take delivery of the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you failed to take delivery within a reasonable time after the request?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I liable to the seller for not taking delivery` IS
    `s 37(1) the liability for neglect or refusal to take delivery applies` `the seller is ready and willing to deliver the goods` `the seller requests me to take delivery` `I do not within a reasonable time after the request take delivery of the goods`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `What the seller may do about goods I have not paid for (ss 38 to 48)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 38(1).  The seller is an unpaid seller when the whole price has not been paid or tendered, or when a bill of exchange or other negotiable instrument was received as conditional payment and the condition has not been fulfilled.
@export Is the seller an unpaid seller (s 38(1))?
GIVEN `the whole of the price has not been paid or tendered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the whole of the price not been paid or tendered?
      `a bill of exchange or other negotiable instrument has been received as conditional payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the seller receive a bill of exchange or other negotiable instrument as conditional payment?
      `the condition on which it was received has not been fulfilled by reason of the dishonour of the instrument or otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the condition on which it was received not been fulfilled, through dishonour of the instrument or otherwise?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the seller an unpaid seller` IS
    `s 38(1) the seller of goods is an unpaid seller` `the whole of the price has not been paid or tendered` `a bill of exchange or other negotiable instrument has been received as conditional payment` `the condition on which it was received has not been fulfilled by reason of the dishonour of the instrument or otherwise`

-- s 41(1).  An unpaid seller in possession of the goods may retain them until payment or tender of the price where they were sold without any stipulation as to credit, where the term of credit has expired, or where the buyer becomes insolvent.
@export May the seller keep the goods until I pay (s 41(1))?
GIVEN `the seller is an unpaid seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller an unpaid seller?
      `the seller is in possession of the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the seller still in possession of the goods?
      `the goods have been sold without any stipulation as to credit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the goods sold without any stipulation as to credit?
      `the goods have been sold on credit but the term of credit has expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Were the goods sold on credit, and has the term of credit expired?
      `I have become insolvent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you become insolvent (ceased to pay your debts in the ordinary course of business, or cannot pay them as they become due)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the seller keep the goods until I pay` IS
    `s 41(1) the unpaid seller is entitled to retain possession of the goods until payment or tender of the price` `the seller is an unpaid seller` `the seller is in possession of the goods` `the goods have been sold without any stipulation as to credit` `the goods have been sold on credit but the term of credit has expired` `I have become insolvent`

-- s 44.  When the buyer becomes insolvent, an unpaid seller who has parted with possession has the right of stopping the goods in transit.
@export May the seller stop the goods in transit (s 44)?
GIVEN `I have become insolvent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you become insolvent?
      `the unpaid seller has parted with the possession of the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the unpaid seller parted with possession of the goods?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the seller stop the goods in transit` IS
    `s 44 the unpaid seller has the right of stopping the goods in transit` `I have become insolvent` `the unpaid seller has parted with the possession of the goods`

-- s 48(3).  Where the goods are perishable, or the seller gives notice of intention to re-sell and the buyer does not pay or tender the price within a reasonable time, the unpaid seller may re-sell and recover damages for any loss.
@export May the seller re-sell the goods and claim damages from me (s 48(3))?
GIVEN `the goods are of a perishable nature` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are the goods perishable?
      `the unpaid seller gives notice to me of his intention to re-sell` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller given you notice of his intention to re-sell?
      `I do not within a reasonable time pay or tender the price` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you failed to pay or tender the price within a reasonable time?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the seller re-sell the goods and claim damages` IS
    `s 48(3) the unpaid seller may re-sell the goods and recover from the original buyer damages for any loss occasioned by his breach of contract` `the goods are of a perishable nature` `the unpaid seller gives notice to me of his intention to re-sell` `I do not within a reasonable time pay or tender the price`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `What I can claim and what the seller can claim (ss 49 to 54)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- ss 49(1) and (2).  Gathered by hand: the seller may sue for the price where the property has passed and the buyer wrongfully neglects or refuses to pay, or where the price is payable on a day certain irrespective of delivery and the buyer wrongfully neglects or refuses to pay.
@export Can the seller sue me for the price (ss 49(1) and (2))?
GIVEN `the property in the goods has passed to me under the contract of sale` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the property in the goods passed to you under the contract?
      `the price is payable on a day certain irrespective of delivery` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the price payable on a day certain irrespective of delivery?
      `I wrongfully neglect or refuse to pay for the goods according to the terms of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you wrongfully neglect or refuse to pay according to the terms of the contract?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the seller sue me for the price` IS
    `ss 49(1) and (2) the seller may maintain an action against the buyer for the price` (`s 49(1) the seller may maintain an action against the buyer for the price of the goods` `the property in the goods has passed to me under the contract of sale` `I wrongfully neglect or refuse to pay for the goods according to the terms of the contract`) (`s 49(2) the seller may maintain an action for the price although the property in the goods has not passed and the goods have not been appropriated to the contract` `the price is payable on a day certain irrespective of delivery` `I wrongfully neglect or refuse to pay for the goods according to the terms of the contract`)

-- s 50(3).  Where there is an available market, the prima facie measure of the seller's damages for non-acceptance is the difference between the contract price and the market price at the time they ought to have been accepted.  Where the market price is above the contract price the text is silent.
@export How much can the seller claim if I wrongly refuse the goods (s 50(3))?
GIVEN `I wrongfully neglect or refuse to accept and pay for the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you wrongfully neglect or refuse to accept and pay for the goods?
      `there is an available market for the goods in question` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there an available market for the goods?
      `the contrary is shown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there evidence that the loss is not the market difference?
      `the contract price` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the contract price, in dollars?
      `the market or current price of the goods at the time they ought to have been accepted` IS A NUMBER @desc What was the market price when they ought to have been accepted, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `How much can the seller claim if I wrongly refuse the goods` IS
    `s 50(3) the measure of damages for non-acceptance where there is an available market` `I wrongfully neglect or refuse to accept and pay for the goods` `there is an available market for the goods in question` `the contrary is shown` `the contract price` `the market or current price of the goods at the time they ought to have been accepted`

-- s 50(3), yes or no.  The question above gives the amount; this one says whether the rule applies at all.
@export Does the market-price rule for the seller's damages apply (s 50(3))?
GIVEN `I wrongfully neglect or refuse to accept and pay for the goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Do you wrongfully neglect or refuse to accept and pay for the goods?
      `there is an available market for the goods in question` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there an available market for the goods?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the market-price rule for the seller's damages apply` IS
    `s 50(3) the market-price rule for non-acceptance applies` `I wrongfully neglect or refuse to accept and pay for the goods` `there is an available market for the goods in question`

-- s 51(1).  Where the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver, the buyer may sue for damages for non-delivery.
@export Can I sue the seller for damages if he does not deliver (s 51(1))?
GIVEN `the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver the goods to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the seller wrongfully neglect or refuse to deliver the goods to you?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I sue the seller for damages for non-delivery` IS
    `s 51(1) the buyer may maintain an action against the seller for damages for non-delivery` `the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver the goods to me`

-- s 51(3).  Where there is an available market, the prima facie measure is the difference between the contract price and the market price at the time the goods ought to have been delivered.
@export How much can I claim if the seller does not deliver (s 51(3))?
GIVEN `the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver the goods to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the seller wrongfully neglect or refuse to deliver the goods to you?
      `there is an available market for the goods in question` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there an available market for the goods?
      `the contrary is shown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there evidence that the loss is not the market difference?
      `the contract price` IS A NUMBER @desc What is the contract price, in dollars?
      `the market or current price of the goods at the time they ought to have been delivered` IS A NUMBER @desc What was the market price when they ought to have been delivered, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `How much can I claim if the seller does not deliver` IS
    `s 51(3) the measure of damages for non-delivery where there is an available market` `the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver the goods to me` `there is an available market for the goods in question` `the contrary is shown` `the contract price` `the market or current price of the goods at the time they ought to have been delivered`

-- s 51(3), yes or no.  The question above gives the amount; this one says whether the rule applies at all.
@export Does the market-price rule for my damages apply (s 51(3))?
GIVEN `the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver the goods to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the seller wrongfully neglect or refuse to deliver the goods to you?
      `there is an available market for the goods in question` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there an available market for the goods?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the market-price rule for my damages apply` IS
    `s 51(3) the market-price rule for non-delivery applies` `the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver the goods to me` `there is an available market for the goods in question`

-- s 52(1).  In an action for breach of a contract to deliver specific or ascertained goods the court may, on the claimant's application, direct that the contract be performed specifically.  Whether the court thinks fit is for the court.
@export Can the court order the seller to deliver the specific goods (s 52(1))?
GIVEN `the action is for breach of contract to deliver specific or ascertained goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the action for breach of a contract to deliver specific or ascertained goods?
      `I apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you applied for specific performance?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the court order specific performance` IS
    `s 52(1) the court has power to direct that the contract shall be performed specifically` `the action is for breach of contract to deliver specific or ascertained goods` `I apply`

-- s 53(1).  A buyer who has only a breach of warranty (or who elects or is compelled to treat a breach of condition as one) is not entitled to reject the goods by reason only of that breach.
@export If the seller has only broken a warranty, may I reject the goods (s 53(1))?
GIVEN `there is a breach of warranty by the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller broken a warranty?
      `I elect or am compelled to treat a breach of a condition on the part of the seller as a breach of warranty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you elected, or are you compelled, to treat a breach of condition as a breach of warranty?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `If the seller has only broken a warranty may I reject the goods` IS
    `s 53(1) the buyer is entitled to reject the goods by reason only of the breach of warranty` `there is a breach of warranty by the seller` `I elect or am compelled to treat a breach of a condition on the part of the seller as a breach of warranty`

-- s 53(3).  For a breach of warranty of quality the loss is prima facie the difference between the value of the goods at delivery and the value they would have had if they had fulfilled the warranty.
@export How much can I claim for goods of lower quality than the warranty (s 53(3))?
GIVEN `there is a breach of warranty of quality` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there a breach of warranty of quality?
      `the contrary is shown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there evidence that the loss is not that difference?
      `the value of the goods at the time of delivery to me` IS A NUMBER @desc What was the value of the goods when delivered to you, in dollars?
      `the value the goods would have had if they had fulfilled the warranty` IS A NUMBER @desc What value would they have had if they had fulfilled the warranty, in dollars?
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
DECIDE `How much can I claim for a breach of warranty of quality` IS
    `s 53(3) the loss for breach of warranty of quality` `there is a breach of warranty of quality` `the contrary is shown` `the value of the goods at the time of delivery to me` `the value the goods would have had if they had fulfilled the warranty`

-- s 53(3), yes or no.  The question above gives the amount; this one says whether the rule applies at all.
@export Does the rule for my damages for a breach of warranty of quality apply (s 53(3))?
GIVEN `there is a breach of warranty of quality` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is there a breach of warranty of quality?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the rule for breach of warranty of quality apply` IS
    `s 53(3) the rule for breach of warranty of quality applies` `there is a breach of warranty of quality`

-- Gathered by hand (E1 option B).  The buyer's damages claim lies for non-delivery (s 51(1)) or for breach of warranty (s 53(1)(b)).
@export Can I claim damages from the seller (ss 51(1) and 53(1)(b))?
GIVEN `the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver the goods to me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the seller wrongfully neglect or refuse to deliver the goods to you?
      `there is a breach of warranty by the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the seller broken a warranty?
      `I elect or am compelled to treat a breach of a condition on the part of the seller as a breach of warranty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you elected, or are you compelled, to treat a breach of condition as a breach of warranty?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I claim damages from the seller` IS
    `route (E1 B) the buyer may maintain an action against the seller for damages` (`s 51(1) the buyer may maintain an action against the seller for damages for non-delivery` `the seller wrongfully neglects or refuses to deliver the goods to me`) (`s 53(1)(b) the buyer may maintain an action against the seller for damages for the breach of warranty` `there is a breach of warranty by the seller` `I elect or am compelled to treat a breach of a condition on the part of the seller as a breach of warranty`)

-- Gathered by hand (E1 option B).  The right to reject the goods comes from a breach of an implied condition (ss 12(1), 13(1), 14(2), 14(3), 15(2)) or from a wrong quantity (s 30(1), (2)), and is cut off by ss 11(3), 15A(1) and 53(1).  Each answer below is your reading of that provision on your facts: Yes, No, or I am not sure.
@export Do I have the right to reject the goods (ss 11 to 15A, 30, 35A, 53)?
GIVEN `section 12(1) has been broken` IS A `My answer` @desc Has the seller broken the implied condition about his right to sell (s 12(1))?
      `section 13(1) has been broken` IS A `My answer` @desc Have the goods failed to match their description (s 13(1))?
      `section 14(2) has been broken` IS A `My answer` @desc Are the goods not of satisfactory quality (s 14(2))?
      `section 14(3) has been broken` IS A `My answer` @desc Are the goods not reasonably fit for the purpose you made known (s 14(3))?
      `section 15(2)(a) has been broken` IS A `My answer` @desc Does the bulk not match the sample in quality (s 15(2)(a))?
      `section 15(2)(b) has been broken` IS A `My answer` @desc Do the goods have a hidden defect that a reasonable examination of the sample would not show (s 15(2)(b))?
      `section 30(1) lets me reject` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the quantity delivered less than you contracted for (s 30(1))?
      `section 30(2) lets me reject` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the quantity delivered more than you contracted for (s 30(2))?
      `section 11(3) limits me to a claim for breach of warranty` IS A `My answer` @desc Have you accepted the goods in a contract that is not severable, so that section 11(3) limits you to a claim for breach of warranty?
      `section 15A(1) limits me to a claim for breach of warranty` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the breach so slight, and you not a consumer, that section 15A(1) limits you to a claim for breach of warranty?
      `the only breach is of a warranty` IS A `My answer` @desc Is the only breach a breach of warranty (including a breach of condition you elected or were compelled to treat as one) (s 53(1))?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I have the right to reject the goods` IS
    `route (E1 B) the buyer has the right to reject the goods` (`what I say, as a fact` `section 12(1) has been broken`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 13(1) has been broken`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 14(2) has been broken`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 14(3) has been broken`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 15(2)(a) has been broken`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 15(2)(b) has been broken`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 30(1) lets me reject`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 30(2) lets me reject`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 11(3) limits me to a claim for breach of warranty`) (`what I say, as a fact` `section 15A(1) limits me to a claim for breach of warranty`) (`what I say, as the Act's no, if yes` `the only breach is of a warranty`)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Excluding terms, auctions and the rest (ss 55 to 62)`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- s 55(1).  A right, duty or liability implied by law may (subject to the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977) be negatived or varied by express agreement, course of dealing, or usage that binds both parties.
@export Can the seller exclude the terms the Act implies (s 55(1))?
GIVEN `a right, duty or liability would arise under the contract by implication of law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Would the right, duty or liability arise under the contract only by implication of law?
      `the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 prevents the negativing or variation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 prevent it?
      `the way` IS A `Way of negativing or varying a right, duty or liability implied by law` @desc In what way did the seller try to exclude or vary it?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the implied terms be excluded` IS
    `s 55(1) the right, duty or liability may be negatived or varied` `a right, duty or liability would arise under the contract by implication of law` `the Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 prevents the negativing or variation` `the way`

-- s 55(2).  An express condition or warranty does not negative a condition or warranty implied by the Act unless inconsistent with it.
@export Does an express term cancel a term the Act implies (s 55(2))?
GIVEN `the express condition or warranty is inconsistent with the condition or warranty implied by this Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the express condition or warranty inconsistent with the implied one?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does an express term cancel an implied term` IS
    `s 55(2) an express condition or warranty negatives a condition or warranty implied by this Act` `the express condition or warranty is inconsistent with the condition or warranty implied by this Act`

-- s 57(2).  A sale by auction is complete when the auctioneer announces completion by the fall of the hammer, or in other customary manner; until then any bidder may retract his bid.
@export Is the auction sale complete, and may I still retract my bid (s 57(2))?
GIVEN `the auctioneer has announced the completion of the sale` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the auctioneer announced the completion of the sale by the fall of the hammer or in another customary manner?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I still retract my bid at the auction` IS
    `s 57(2) any bidder may retract his bid` `the auctioneer has announced the completion of the sale`

-- s 57(4).  Where a sale by auction is not notified to be subject to a right to bid by or on behalf of the seller, it is not lawful for the seller to bid himself or to employ any person to bid.
@export Is it lawful for the seller to bid at the auction (s 57(4))?
GIVEN `the sale by auction is notified to be subject to a right to bid by or on behalf of the seller` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the sale notified to be subject to a right to bid by or on behalf of the seller?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it lawful for the seller to bid at the auction` IS
    `s 57(4) it is lawful for the seller to bid himself or to employ any person to bid at the sale` `the sale by auction is notified to be subject to a right to bid by or on behalf of the seller`

-- s 57(6).  Where a right to bid is expressly reserved (but not otherwise) the seller or any one person on his behalf may bid.
@export May the seller, or one person for him, bid at the auction (s 57(6))?
GIVEN `a right to bid is expressly reserved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was a right to bid expressly reserved?
      `the seller bids himself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the seller bid himself?
      `the number of persons who bid on his behalf` IS A NUMBER @desc How many persons bid on the seller's behalf?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the seller or one person for him bid at the auction` IS
    `s 57(6) the seller, or any one person on his behalf, may bid at the auction` `a right to bid is expressly reserved` `the seller bids himself` `the number of persons who bid on his behalf`

-- s 62(2).  The rules of the common law apply to contracts for the sale of goods, except in so far as they are inconsistent with the provisions of the Act.
@export Does a rule of the common law apply to my contract (s 62(2))?
GIVEN `the contract is a contract for the sale of goods` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contract a contract for the sale of goods?
      `the rule of the common law is inconsistent with the provisions of this Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the rule of the common law inconsistent with the provisions of the Act?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does a rule of the common law apply to my contract` IS
    `s 62(2) the rule of the common law applies to the contract` `the contract is a contract for the sale of goods` `the rule of the common law is inconsistent with the provisions of this Act`

-- s 62(4).  The Act's provisions about contracts of sale do not apply to a transaction in the form of a contract of sale intended to operate by way of mortgage, pledge, charge, or other security.
@export Does the Act apply to a sale that is really a security (s 62(4))?
GIVEN `the transaction is in the form of a contract of sale which is intended to operate by way of mortgage, pledge, charge, or other security` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the transaction in the form of a contract of sale, but intended to operate as a mortgage, pledge, charge or other security?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act apply to a sale that is really a security` IS
    `s 62(4) the provisions of this Act about contracts of sale apply to the transaction` `the transaction is in the form of a contract of sale which is intended to operate by way of mortgage, pledge, charge, or other security`
